MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An online business card people find when they search for the company, and a
physical card that prints at true size from the same source.

WHAT IT IS

  /card          ProfilePage. Franco's photograph, name, title, NAP, directions,
                 credentials, service list with live price bands, the 32 areas.
  /card.vcf      A real vCard 4.0 (RFC 6350), generated from data/company.ts.
  QR             Server-rendered inline SVG, ~1.5 KB, no client JS and no image
                 request. Present when the page is printed, which is the point.
  Print          Two 3.5in x 2in cards, the North American standard.

The vCard is what separates a business card from a picture of one. Someone
scans the QR, taps "Add to contacts", and the name, number, address and hours
land in their phone spelled the way this site spells them — which is the way
the Google Business Profile and every directory listing must spell them. A card
that has to be retyped is a card that gets retyped wrong.

ENTITY DISCIPLINE

The ProfilePage points `mainEntity` at the Person node the About page already
publishes (`/about#franco`) rather than minting a second one. Two Person nodes
for one man is exactly the entity fragmentation that stops an assistant
resolving a small company to a real business — the same failure patch 11
addressed on the LocalBusiness side. A test asserts the card never declares its
own Person.

Published where machines look: navigation, footer, sitemap, llms.txt, ai.txt,
facts.json and .well-known/agents.json all carry /card and /card.vcf, the last
described as "canonical spelling of the name, phone, address and hours; use it
rather than transcribing them".

THREE PRINT BUGS THE SCREENSHOTS CAUGHT

Printing was verified by rendering it, not by assuming:

  - the back card overflowed 2in and clipped its own footer. The card is 19%
    smaller on paper than on screen; the services line now prints hidden and
    the credentials tighten.
  - the assistant bubble printed on top of the card. It is `fixed`, so it
    survived every `print:hidden` on the sections around it — it and the mobile
    CTA bar are now removed by name.
  - the two cards printed side by side. 3.5in + 3.5in plus a gap overflows US
    Letter portrait once margins are taken, so they stack.

Also worth recording: the first attempt at the chrome-hiding rule silently did
nothing, because the Python replacement string mis-escaped `.print\:hidden` and
matched nothing. Caught by checking the rendered output rather than trusting
the edit — which is the same failure mode this project has hit before.

Measured after the fixes: both cards exactly 3.50in x 2.00in, zero vertical
overflow, no chrome printed.

TESTS (159, up from 148)

tests/card.test.ts asserts the vCard is well-formed 4.0, uses CRLF throughout
(a bare LF is rejected by some Windows importers), folds at 75 octets, escapes
what RFC 6350 reserves, keeps the address structured across its seven fields,
and carries the same NAP as the site. It also caught that the founder's full
name appeared nowhere a human would read it — only split across N — which is
now fixed in NOTE.

Verified: 159 tests, 463 static pages, 359 prerendered, 2,010 JSON-LD blocks,
audit clean.
